    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "The **potent patch-clamp result should drive the decision**, provisionally.\n\nWhy:\n\n- Patch clamp measures the functional endpoint that matters: inhibition of hERG-mediated potassium current.\n- Radioligand displacement measures binding to the assay\u2019s ligand site, not necessarily channel block. hERG blockers can show weak displacement because of state dependence, different binding sites, membrane conditions, or assay artifacts.\n- Cisapride\u2019s established pharmacology is consistent with clinically important hERG blockade and QT risk, so the weak binding result should not override functional evidence.\n\nBefore finalising, confirm that both studies used the same cisapride form and that the patch-clamp result is reproducible, concentration-response based, and conducted under relevant conditions. Compare its IC50 with the **unbound clinical exposure**. If the functional IC50 is near or below relevant exposure, treat cisapride as a hERG liability and make a conservative progression decision.",
